  "help": "<p>This wrapper exposes selected PhyKIT tree and alignment metrics.</p>\n<div class=\"section\" id=\"input-modes\">\n<h1>Input modes</h1>\n<p>Single-file mode preserves the direct PhyKIT output for one tree or alignment.\nCollection mode runs the selected metric for each member of one Galaxy\ncollection and writes a combined table. Grouped-collection mode accepts repeated\ngroup labels plus collections and writes a long table suitable for downstream\ngroup comparison tools.</p>\n<p>ZIP archive modes are convenience inputs for archive-heavy histories. They\nextract local Galaxy ZIP datasets inside the job working directory, reject unsafe\nmember paths such as absolute paths or <tt class=\"docutils literal\">..</tt> traversal, apply the same\nsuffix/regular-expression filters, and then run the selected PhyKIT metric.\nGalaxy-native collection mode remains the preferred route when users want to\ninspect or reuse extracted archive members.</p>\n<p>Collection outputs include:</p>\n<ul class=\"simple\">\n<li>a metrics table with group, file identifier, file name, metric, value, and\nstatus columns;</li>\n<li>a failed-files table with nonzero or unparsable PhyKIT runs; and</li>\n<li>a summary table with total, selected, processed, failed, and skipped file\ncounts per group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use suffix or regular-expression filters when a collection contains mixed files,\nfor example to process only <tt class=\"docutils literal\">.treefile</tt> members for tree metrics. In ZIP\nmodes, leaving both filters blank processes every regular archive member and\nwrites a warning to standard error.</p>\n</div>\n",
